I am Erin forward and I am your Attorney  General I'm honored to be here today to  speak to you about Juneteenth today in  which accordance to our own state laws  passed by sponsored by and passed by  then a similar man Harvey Mumford a day  that we commemorate the abolition of  slavery in the United States  Jim taint has an interesting history and  while we celebrate in response to  President Lincoln signing the  Emancipation Proclamation it's important  to note that abolishing slavery was  somewhat of an afterthought if you know  me I just speak the truth  I appreciate the altruistic outcomes but  sometimes altruistic outcomes are  brought about as a matter of necessity  when the American Civil War started in  1861 President Lincoln made it clear  that his only goal at the time was to  preserve the Union in fact in a letter  to Horace Greeley who is the founder and  editor of the New York Tribune President  Lincoln said this and I quote my  paramount object in this struggle is to  save the Union  and it is not either to save or destroy  slavery if I could save the Union  without freeing any slave I would do it  and if I could save it by freeing all  the slaves I would do it and if I could  save it by freeing some and leaving  others alone I would do that too it  eventually obviously became obvious that  preserving the Union would mean the  destruction of slavery but during the  early part of the war captured slaves  were actually held as contraband of war  with the actual expectation that they  would eventually be returned to their  owners on January 1 1863 however  President Lincoln took the critical step  of issue  the Emancipation Proclamation the order  freed the slaves in areas occupied by  Union armies but it did not immediately  affect those who lived in the ten states  of the Confederacy and so on June 19th  this day in 1865 two and a half years  after the signing of the Emancipation  Proclamation the union's Major General  Gordon Granger arrived in Galveston  Texas with 2,000 federal troops he  assemble those who were present and he  read general order number three which  informed the people of Texas including  my fourth great-grandmother and my third  great-grandparents I imagine they were  in Texas at the time in accordance with  the Emancipation Proclamation that the  slaves for free as you might imagine the  damned former slaves received the news  of great joy spontaneously organizing a  celebration which was the first  celebration of the end of slavery in its  entirety in the United States as the  Union troops advanced the joyous news  reached more and more people one of them  you might note Booker T Washington he at  the time was a nine year old boy living  in Virginia and he recalled the events  as follows and I quote as the great day  grew nearer there was more singing in  the slave quarters than usual it was  bolder and more rain and lasted later  into the night most of the verses of the  plantation songs had some reference to  freedom some man who seemed to be a  stranger a union a United States officer  I presume made a little speech and then  read a rather long paper the  Emancipation Proclamation I think after  the reading we were told that we were  all free and could go when and where we  pleased my mother who was standing by my  side leaned over and kissed her children  while tears of joy ran down her cheek  she explained to us what it all meant  that this was a day for which she had  been so long praying but fearing that  she would never live to see since that  time June 10th has become an important  annual tradition in many parts of the  country it's a day to celebrate our  ancestors our heritage and our history  but as we celebrate June 10th today we  need to remember that the Emancipation  Proclamation and the victory of the  Union in the Civil War marked the end of  the institution of slavery but the  struggle for true freedom and equality  was just beginning we know this to be  true especially during these times we  are reminded and frankly I'm reminded  both mentally and physically I literally  have a headache and it won't go away the  recent killing of George Floyd at the  hands of police officers has reopened  wounds wounds that have never really  healed and in fact sometimes they  haven't even begun to scalp police  brutality against black Americans as  both age-old and current thus as a  member of law enforcement who  appreciates the sacrifice of my  colleagues and as a member of society  who believes in fairness and  accountability I embrace and acknowledge  the fact that conversations around  police reform or entirely appropriate  and timely but as I've said before the  time for conversing should be near over  if not already completed we should  already be seeing tangible policies and  the consistent enforcement of those  policies to create to restore in to  augment trust between members of law  enforcement and the communities that  they serve  indeed members of the public have the  absolute rights to believe that the type  of interaction that they experience with  law enforcement will not depend  the color of their skin in that if an  injustice occurs the response will be a  fair and impartial netting of justice  that's why I'm proud of the  conversations conclusions and  suggestions and solutions revealed thus  far during our panels up the justice and  injustice forum that I began a few weeks  back  many who have participated have outlined  policies they recommend it or have  already instituted in the wake of George  horse killing time which we do not have  and for which we should not have to wait  will tell if these forums will lead to  results I'm optimistic that said I'd  like to share my vulnerability during  these times like these every time this  happens and I get goose bumps you can  see them right now these are bad goose  bumps  they always caused me to exist in an  uncomfortable consistent persistent and  virtually perpetual state of anxiety in  part because I sense our country  simultaneously being on the precipice of  both progress and pandemonium and you  never know which way we're going to go  we're the protests in what's being  sought through them be considered and  received with open minds and thereafter  acting upon or will the naysayers and  those who oppose actual progress hone in  on the actions of a few who attempt to  co-opt the conversation to divert  attention away from it  to undermine it and to drown out the  concerns being raised will people and  politicians take these concerns  seriously and respond with changes in  the enforcement of laws policies and  practices or will they bide their time  and actually do what they've done before  which is wait for the fervor to die down  and simply continue with the status quo  again  only time will tell but honestly as we  as we wait for time to tell anxiety  continues to permeate parts of my my  life because I'm forced to renew my  personal concerns for my son's safety my  son's I have three of them and a nephew  that we've raised and I worry about  their safety in not to mention my wife's  and my sanity we're scared saw my sons  these are emotions I don't wish on  anyone yet these emotions are  consistently recurring and they appear  to be chronic in my life and I suspect  the lives of most black Americans to say  however that these concerns are born  purely out of personal or vicarious  experiences with law enforcement belies  the truth and it minimizes if not wholly  negates the fact that this is a systemic  problem we must address the fact that  we've created an entire criminal justice  system that statistics show for example  discriminatorily applies discretion  and/or benefit of the doubt when it  comes to whether an officer arrests an  individual whether a prosecutor charges  him and if so with what and if a judge  sentences him and if so to what reform  of the entire criminal justice system  therefore is a key and necessary  component of this conversation about  systemic racism but beyond the criminal  justice system we must also address the  fact that similar discriminatory  practices and policies in other aspects  of the system of our democracy contain  systemic racism as well to deny that  fact likewise negates the  well-documented discriminatory practices  that exist in our education economic  housing and employment systems just to  name a few  and it also ignores the fact that our  political system also shares blame  and perpetuating systemic racism I'm  about to share some things that may have  me losing friends maybe even some  acquaintances as well but some of our  elected leaders continue to contribute  to the problem either actively or  passively or wittingly or unwittingly  and sometimes all of the above I don't  have to talk about politicians outside  of Nevada to provide examples and  frankly I don't have to go back decades  to find these examples each event that  I'm about to describe to you has  happened that since I was first elected  to public office in 2012 in fact I wrote  about many of them in a 2013 opinion  piece that I wrote entitled a  conversation all too common I mean  speaking of slavery which today is the  day that we commemorate the abolition of  it consider the fact that I once had to  explain to my sons while a former  legislative colleague of mine was taped  saying that if his constituents wanted  him to keep vote to bring back slavery  the quintessential example of a racist  system  further consider that I've also had to  discuss with my sons while another  legislative colleague of mine would have  the audacity to as he says provocatively  right  in essence that blacks aren't grateful  enough to whites for the roles in ending  slavery and then consider that I also  had to talk to my sons about yet another  legislative colleague of mine who said  on the radio regarding an upcoming  election at the time that he actually  hoped that minorities and young folks  wouldn't vote even though exercising the  right to vote is undoubtedly one of the  best ways to help dismantle systemic  racism and then consider the fact that  each of these former colleagues of mine  were either or would later become  elected leaders of their legislative  caucus they maybe you'll understand a  little bit why I have this perpetual  headache to their credit the colleagues  about which I speak now apologized for  their comments two of them actually  apologized to me directly and one even  called me to ask that I relay his  apology to my sons  that's a judgment in my estimation was  so compromised that they could even  allow themselves to utter those words  however is problematic and in my  estimation it contributes to a systemic  racism in ways that we see and that we  don't see at the end maybe the aspirant  of equality will soothe my headache and  maybe one day I'll be able to cope  better right now though it feels like a  migraine and it keeps me up at night and  it wakes me up in the morning it's  unfair it's unjust it's improper and  it's problematic not to mention painful  now make no mistake about it I'll show  my vulnerability not to wine but to let  you know that I remain undaunted but the  showing today represents an opportunity  of a way forward and I'm encouraged by  you being here today to celebrate  Juneteenth and also to speak out against  recent events I ask that you continue  doing that and know that I stand with  you so again thank you for so much for  having me out Shelly Buerkle Thank You  total
 

